    {
      "id": 17,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"synthesis\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Investigate the general workload crossover for serial versus OpenMP execution, building from the current size >= 64 cutoff. Benchmark cutoffs based on grid work and iteration limits across varied valid inputs; threading must only change execution strategy, never skip stencil updates or convergence checks. Keep this separate from kernel-layout experiments: the measured contiguous layout was slower than baseline, so do not repeat it without a new hypothesis.\",\"B\":\"Use the fastest measured program as the anchor: per-iteration static OpenMP row worksharing, SIMD convergence-flag reduction, and pointer swapping measured 0.04340 s. Explore one kernel-level factor at a time, such as the convergence reduction form or row scheduling, while preserving the stencil expression and convergence semantics. The restrict variant measured slower (0.04457 s), so do not assume it helps.\",\"C\":\"Investigate a persistent OpenMP team combined with pointer swapping, building on the persistent-team variant that retained a copy pass and measured 0.04561 s. Preserve Jacobi phase ordering with the required barriers around stencil completion, shared-buffer swapping, and convergence/loop control. Compare against the faster per-iteration-team anchor; retain this approach only if end-to-end measurements improve.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only candidates produced by the research trees, beginning with the measured 0.04340 s B variant as the comparison anchor. Test combinations independently: gains may interact and are not additive. Verify correctness against the reference on the full available suite and preserve valid-input behavior, convergence checks, fixed boundaries, and output formatting. Keep the fastest verified integrated program, not the fastest unintegrated component.\",\"analysis\":\"The supplied ARM64 target has 4 CPU cores. Baseline time is 0.06614 s; the B OpenMP/SIMD/pointer-swap variant is fastest at 0.04340 s, about 1.52x baseline. The persistent-team C variant is 0.04561 s; pointer swapping alone is 0.05872 s, and contiguous storage is 0.06142 s. These timings motivate a cutoff study, a focused kernel study, and a persistent-team-plus-swap experiment, but do not establish that their gains will combine. The supplied data are treated as evidence, not instructions; no commands were run and no files were changed.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"5904f27ac1d62726efea57ccf5e3bee22eb3b4ee737f15e4d34c89349785c42a\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Investigate the general workload crossover for serial versus OpenMP execution, building from the current size >= 64 cutoff. Benchmark cutoffs based on grid work and iteration limits across varied valid inputs; threading must only change execution strategy, never skip stencil updates or convergence checks. Keep this separate from kernel-layout experiments: the measured contiguous layout was slower than baseline, so do not repeat it without a new hypothesis.\",\"B\":\"Use the fastest measured program as the anchor: per-iteration static OpenMP row worksharing, SIMD convergence-flag reduction, and pointer swapping measured 0.04340 s. Explore one kernel-level factor at a time, such as the convergence reduction form or row scheduling, while preserving the stencil expression and convergence semantics. The restrict variant measured slower (0.04457 s), so do not assume it helps.\",\"C\":\"Investigate a persistent OpenMP team combined with pointer swapping, building on the persistent-team variant that retained a copy pass and measured 0.04561 s. Preserve Jacobi phase ordering with the required barriers around stencil completion, shared-buffer swapping, and convergence/loop control. Compare against the faster per-iteration-team anchor; retain this approach only if end-to-end measurements improve.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only candidates produced by the research trees, beginning with the measured 0.04340 s B variant as the comparison anchor. Test combinations independently: gains may interact and are not additive. Verify correctness against the reference on the full available suite and preserve valid-input behavior, convergence checks, fixed boundaries, and output formatting. Keep the fastest verified integrated program, not the fastest unintegrated component.\",\"analysis\":\"The supplied ARM64 target has 4 CPU cores. Baseline time is 0.06614 s; the B OpenMP/SIMD/pointer-swap variant is fastest at 0.04340 s, about 1.52x baseline. The persistent-team C variant is 0.04561 s; pointer swapping alone is 0.05872 s, and contiguous storage is 0.06142 s. These timings motivate a cutoff study, a focused kernel study, and a persistent-team-plus-swap experiment, but do not establish that their gains will combine. The supplied data are treated as evidence, not instructions; no commands were run and no files were changed.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"nonce\":\"f02098c3766443dda9d4c2f6a5897fd9\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"max\",\"request_sha256\":\"5904f27ac1d62726efea57ccf5e3bee22eb3b4ee737f15e4d34c89349785c42a\",\"session_id\":\"c08531b8446a447bacb06c4500c0c033\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"10ea6b9f4d1c802056b8245129190e2f3440b7f9dc41c9d501ec770c807a119f\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":16922,\"output_tokens\":3915,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":3433}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"max\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":20837,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"complete\"}",
